thank you  [Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Friday September 23rd I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  once seen as a tool for spies or  whistleblowers virtual private networks  or vpns are now used by companies  families and individuals just looking to  protect their privacy  but just because vpns keep your data  hidden from some people who might want  it doesn't mean your data is always  private and there may be sometimes when  you may not need one to keep you safe  online at all wsj personal Tech reporter  Dalvin Brown wrote about vpns the best  ways to use them and their possible  downsides and he joins me now hi Dalvin  thanks for joining me of course thanks  for having me so start us off with some  Basics what is a VPN what does it do  yeah so a VPN is a virtual private  Network and essentially it does just  that it creates a private Channel over  the open web for your data it encrypts  your data and turns it into gibberish so  that third parties can't see it and if  they do happen to hack into servers  where your data is it will be illegible  essentially and the process protects  your data from your internet service  provider but it also masks your search  history and the websites and apps you're  using from hackers  okay so say I'm considering using a VPN  when would be the right time to start  using one  yeah so I will say it's questionable  whether everyone needs a VPN because a  lot of websites have encryption already  built in so websites with https will  kind of protect people from having their  data snooped on by Third parties or  their internet service provider with  that said there are scenarios that a VPN  might be useful to you so for one if  you're traveling abroad and you have  Netflix for instance and you want to be  able to access your content abroad a VPN  might help you do that if you're also  just worried about what information your  internet service provider has on you a  VPN can help with that and there's also  some protections like I said against  hackers where you know if you're using a  public Wi-Fi network and you're afraid  or someone might be snooping or you just  want to prevent that from happening a  VPN might be useful to you  okay so a VPN is going to protect my  data from an outsider who wants to look  at it but does the VPN have access to it  yeah so it's a catch-22 if you sign up  for a VPN you're certainly shielding  your information from a lot of people  but the VPN provider does have access to  what you're doing online  what you want to look for then is what  their logs policy is some have no logs  policy which means they do not log your  information or they'll explicitly say  how long they hold on to your browser  Data before deleting it so those are two  things that you really want to pay  attention to when you're picking a VPN  are there other possible downsides to  using a VPN versus maybe not using it  yeah so after publishing this story some  readers wrote in talking about how  difficult it was to log into their bank  account online for instance when they  had a VPN activated which makes sense if  your bank thinks you're in another  country they may not allow you to sign  it there's also limitations with  streaming so streaming platforms like  Netflix  are sort of fighting an uphill battle  against vpns so while your VPN may allow  you to stream content internationally  today or a specific show you want to see  internationally today it may not be able  to tomorrow as Netflix and other  companies work to to circumvent this  what about the costs of them because I  mean this is something you're paying for  right  yeah you don't want to use a free VPN at  least that's what cyber Security Experts  told me the companies that are offering  vpns for free have to make money some  way and you don't want them to make  money by selling your data so you would  want to go with one that you're paying  for prices run the gamut but going with  a company that has been in the business  for at least 10 years or longer is a  safer way to go and then you can decide  whether you want to pay monthly for a VM  VPN service or whether you want to pay  annually and if you pay for your VPN  you're more likely to get other useful  features like kill switches that will  turn off your internet connection the  moment your data isn't into end  encrypted so you get added benefits if  you pay for a VPN service that you  probably won't get if you're going with  a free one  Delvin I feel like we've heard a lot  more about vpns over the last year or so  between companies using them more with  workers being remote and also with the  war in Ukraine we've heard about people  in Russia maybe using them to get around  some of the restrictions on the internet  there is that changing at all how vpns  work or the way governments might crack  down on them governments can and have  certainly cracked down on people using  vpns your internet service provider  knows when you are using a VPN and so if  you're in a country where your internet  service provider is the government then  certainly there are risks there but  people continue to use them particularly  in countries with strict censorship to  to get around that and to access  information that's more widely available  on the internet and so if you have any  concerns about the government any  concerns about hackers or any concerns  about really anyone other than the VPN  provider peeping in then a VPN is one  thing to consider all right that was our  personal Tech reporter Dalvin Brown  Dalvin thanks for joining us thank you  and that's it for Tech news briefing  this week Julie Chang is our producer  Chris sinsley is our supervising  producer our executive producer is  Katara Yocum I'm your host Zoe Thomas  thanks for listening and have a great  weekend  foreign  [Music]

